Description:
    A seed file for every model your `recourses` routes serve, so every screen
    has rows to show. Each db/seeds/<resources>.rb holds 25 rows: the first
    carries only what a row cannot save without — whatever a presence or
    inclusion validator demands, and every required belongs_to — the last fills
    every attribute, and the rest mix which optional attributes are filled, so
    a nullable column is seen both ways.

    Values are of each column's own type and nothing more, varied by row so a
    unique index is satisfied: strings are numbered, an email column gets an
    address, a phone column ten valid digits, an enum cycles the words it
    admits, and a reference reads the first row of the model it points at. A
    validator with stricter opinions is yours to satisfy by editing the file.

    db/seeds.rb gains the one line that loads the directory, once, so follow-up
    `rails db:seed` runs pick every file up — alphabetically, so reorder by
    hand where one model's rows need another's to exist first. A resource whose
    model does not exist is skipped, and a file that already exists asks before
    being overwritten.

Example:
    bin/rails generate recourse:seed

    This will create:
        db/seeds/<resources>.rb, one per model `recourses` declares
        the loader line in db/seeds.rb, unless it is already there
